course: dictionary
lesson: 8392
title: Ruddy: one word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. «The farmer's ruddy cheeks showed he had worked all day in the wind and sun.» Here ruddy describes a healthy face that is red or pink from being outdoors.
explain: Ruddy can also simply mean reddish in colour. When you meet it, ask: is the writer describing a face, a colour, or something else? The words around it will tell you.
explain: In everyday British talk, ruddy can be a mild, old-fashioned word used to show annoyance. «The ruddy car won't start» means the speaker is cross, but it is a softer word, a polite stand-in for a ruder one.
fact: Ruddy means healthy-looking and red or pink, often from being outdoors, as in «ruddy cheeks».
fact: Ruddy can also mean reddish in colour.
fact: In UK speech, ruddy can be a mild, old-fashioned word used to show annoyance or to add emphasis.
fact: Ruddy is an adjective (ruddier, ruddiest), and in informal UK speech it can also be an adverb.
fact: Phrases you may meet include «ruddy complexion» and «ruddy hell» (UK, mild).
exercise: The farmer had «ruddy cheeks» from the wind. What does ruddy mean here? | Old and grey | *Healthy and red or pink from being outdoors | Dirty and covered in mud | Blue and very cold
exercise: «The ruddy car won't start!» says Gran, who is cross. What does ruddy show here? | Great happiness | Surprise at a new car | *Mild annoyance | The colour of new paint
exercise: Which word fits this gap: a ___ complexion after a day on the boat? | sour | silent | tiny | *ruddy
test: Which sentence uses ruddy to mean reddish in colour? | The ruddy dog sat quietly by the fire | Ruddy is a short word for a long sentence | *The old brick wall had a ruddy glow in the evening light
test: Ruddy is used in UK talk to show annoyance. What is a good meaning for it when someone says «ruddy» about a broken lamp? | A way to say the lamp is sweet | *A mild, old-fashioned way of showing annoyance | The name of a farm animal | A word for a loud noise
exam: «The ruddy-faced sailor laughed as the wind filled his sails.» What does ruddy-faced suggest about the sailor? | Pale and ill | Angry and rude | *Healthy, with red cheeks from sea wind | Fast asleep
